$.#596
|  I have also argued that any cognitive
|  science, to be comprehensive, has to work
|  with cognition as lived by flesh-and-blood
.  people in social, embodied, personalistic life-worlds
asl-sentence-text $# 596 ;.

asl-new-sentence 4# 592 ;.
asl-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 592 107961 2035 55 ;.
asl-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 592 108237 2041 28 ;.
asl-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#597
|  Such an approach would be only tangentially a
|  natural science --- the physicalistic
|  intuitions that lend systematicity
|  and disputative structure to studies of nature
|  would, in this context, dilute rather than
.  guarantee explanatory rigor
asl-sentence-text $# 597 ;.

asl-new-sentence 4# 593 ;.
asl-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 593 108238 2041 29 ;.
asl-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 593 108410 2045 8 ;.
asl-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#598
|  Endorsing
|  phenomenology as an alternative foundation,
|  theories of cognitive-semantic "staging"
|  would --- on this architecture --- rest on phenomenological
.  grounds
asl-sentence-text $# 598 ;.

asl-new-sentence 4# 594 ;.
asl-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 594 108411 2045 9 ;.
asl-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 594 108626 2050 46 ;.
asl-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#599
|  Thus semantics has a
|  kind of nomological dependence
|  on cognitive phenomenology, and
|  syntax on semantics in the sense
|  that phrase-structure rules become entrenched
.  by virtue of conceptual demands on word-order
asl-sentence-text $# 599 ;.

asl-new-sentence 4# 595 ;.
asl-sentence-switch-pos 4#/4 595 108627 2050 47 ;.
asl-sentence--end-pos 4#/4 595 108793 2055 13 ;.
asl-sentence-end-punctuation $ . ;.
$.#600
|  So phenomenology can stand as a foundation
|  supporting multiple facets of the
|  linguistics project, even if it
|  lies outside the normal scope of that
.  discipline's
asl-sentence-text $# 600 ;.

